---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import UnitsLifePairConverter from '@/components/tools/units/UnitsLifePairConverter.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="feet-to-meters">
  <UnitsLifePairConverter client:load spec="feet-to-meters" />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>Feet and inches in, metres out</h2>
    <p>
      The difficult part of this conversion is not the multiplication; it is that the input is two
      numbers. Heights, room dimensions and ceiling drops come as "five ten" or "8 ft 6 in", and many
      converters require decimal feet first.
    </p>
    <p>
      This feet to meters converter accepts <code>5&apos;10&quot;</code>, <code>5 ft 10 in</code> or
      <code>5.833</code>, and all three give 1.778 m. The page shows how it read your entry, so an
      ambiguous apostrophe cannot change the answer by a factor of twelve without you seeing it.
    </p>

    <h2>The factor</h2>
    <p>
      One foot is exactly 0.3048 m, by definition. Multiply feet by 0.3048 for metres, or by 30.48 for
      centimetres. One inch is exactly 2.54 cm, which is the same figure divided by twelve.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>6 ft = 1.8288 m</li>
      <li>5 ft 10 in = 70 in = 177.8 cm = 1.778 m</li>
      <li>8 ft = 2.4384 m</li>
      <li>100 ft = 30.48 m</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Which unit does the form want?</h2>
    <p>
      For height on a visa, passport, medical or gym form, the answer is almost always centimetres as a
      whole number. Convert once from your feet-and-inches figure and round at the end. Do not convert a
      height already rounded to the nearest inch, because that rounding alone can be up to 1.3 cm.
    </p>
    <p>
      For construction, European drawings use metres to three decimal places (to the millimetre).
    </p>

    <h2>Where feet survive in metric countries</h2>
    <p>
      Aviation is the main case: altitudes and flight levels are in feet almost everywhere, and flight
      level FL350 means 35,000 ft, or 10,668 m. Diving uses metres in most of the world and feet in the US.
      UK climbing and hiking guidebooks give summit heights in feet as often as in metres: Ben Nevis is
      1,345 m or 4,413 ft.
    </p>

    <h2>Squares and cubes convert differently</h2>
    <p>
      A common and expensive mistake: square feet do not convert with 0.3048. Area scales with the square
      of the length ratio, so 1 sq ft = 0.09290304 m&sup2;, and you divide by 10.7639 to go from square
      feet to square metres. Volume uses the cube: 1 cu ft = 0.0283168 m&sup3;. This page converts only
      linear length; on an area figure it would make a room look eleven times too big.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
